                         Zendik's Tale - Part 1
                          [The Journey Begins]

                            by Alan Schwartz


    "Good luck, my boy!" the old man whispered to me as I struggled to
control my tears at our parting. Rentar had brought me up from birth,
when he found me at his doorstep, wrapped in a piece of tattered cloth,
and had been my mentor as well. I knew that I might never see him again,
and I wondered if the magic he had taught me would be enough to ensure
my survival.
    Taking one look back at the only father I had ever known, I slowly
descended the stairs of the ruin. The tales of the caverns below and
the evil Wizard of Yendor who built them were legendary, and I had long
ago decided that I too would seek my fortune in the depths. Perhaps I
might even be the one to find the Amulet of Yendor, I thought, my
tears soon transforming into a cocky half-smile. Yes, surely I would
succeed.
    Rentar had girded me well for the journey, provided me with diverse
tools of a most potent and wizardly nature. For a weapon, he provided me
with a magical athame, blessed by Thoth himself and enchanted to extra
sharpness. From his library, he had provided me with three scrolls and
a spellbook, and instructed me on their use. Three vials hung from my
belt, containing elixirs brewed to increase speed, restore lost
vitality, and allow the drinker to sense other beings, and a wand of
striking was tucked into my pouch. The fine touches were the Rentar's
own cloak, designed to protect against magical attacks, and two rings,
one of which would protect the wearer from flame and the other of which
would allow him to see even hidden creatures.
    Reaching the bottom of the stairs, I blinked as my eyes got used to
the darkness. The walls of the cave glowed slightly with some unearthly
phosphorescence for which I was glad, having neither torch nor lamp to
guide me. No matter, I thought. I am a Wizard now, and such things need
not concern me for long. I grinned to myself.
    A sudden scratching sound behind me startled me out of my reverie. I
spun, my heart racing, prepared to dash for the stairs and safety.
I looked about for the source of the sound. From behind the staircase,
Virgil, my master's black cat, walked brazenly to my feet and nuzzled at
my legs. I laughed, reaching down to pet the cat, who seemed intent on
following me. Very well, I thought.
    Looking about the room, I spied a bottle with some magenta liquid.
Virgil noticed it, too, but seemed to go out of her way to avoid it.
I picked it up, and carefully hung it from my belt with the others.
A passage in the walls of the cave lead north, and I walked toward it,
watching with amusement as Virgil, trailing behind me, pounced on
an egg of some sort. Her contented expression reminded me that I would
have to find food for myself here as well. I had heard tales of other
adventurers having to survive by eating the very meat of monsters, and
hoped I would find more sanitary rations.
    Shuddering, I pressed onward into the unknown.


